es, Inc. develops, manufactures, and distributes life science research and clinical diagnostic products in the United States, Europe, Asia, Canada, Latin America, and internationally. It operates through two segments, Life Science and Clinical Diagnostics. The Life Science segment develops, manufactures, and markets instruments, systems, reagents, and consumables to separate, purify, characterize, and quantify biological materials, including cells, proteins, and nucleic acids used in research and biopharmaceutical laboratory environments, as well as for biopharmaceutical manufacturing, quality control process, food safety, and science education applications. This segment serves universities and medical schools, industrial research organizations, government agencies, pharmaceutical manufacturers, biotechnology companies, food producers, and testing laboratories. Its Clinical Diagnostics segment designs, manufactures, markets, and supports diagnostic test systems, informatics systems, test kits, and specialized quality controls for clinical, hospital, diagnostic reference, and transfusion and physician office laboratories, as well as software. The company offers its products through its direct commercial organization, as well as through distributors, agents, brokers, and resellers. Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc. was founded in 1952 and is headquartered in Hercules, California.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Norman D. Schwartz | Chairman & CEO | Age 75 | Compensation: $1.7M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 7K people. Revenue per employee of $347K ranks in the below the sector median among Healthcare peers. Each employee generates $50K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $8K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 5\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Bio-Rad Laboratories, Inc. is a mid-cap healthcare business with a $9.9B market cap on $2.6B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -3% over the past three years and +2% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests. Gross margins of 55% confirm genuine pricing power.","The business throws off $375M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 15 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, with adequate 0.70\u00d7 cash conversion.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 1.0\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 3.0% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 16% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.2\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 37% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.36615374568856357},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.2% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are overwhelmingly selling (100% sell ratio) \u2014 the people who know the business want less of it","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","ratio":0.0},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Healthcare peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Patent cliffs, drug-pricing reform, and reimbursement pressure \u2014 a single regulatory change can reset the pricing power that justifies the multiple.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"GLP-1 disruption across sub-sectors \u2014 weight-loss drugs are reshaping demand for orthopaedics, cardiovascular devices, and sleep-apnea treatments in ways not yet priced in.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Healthcare sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 13% over 3 months","Healthcare ETF in a sustained uptrend above its long-term average","Strong 6-month sector performance (30.7% return)"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"2026 Global Screener Proficiency Testing Platform Market: $2.01 Billion Forecast for 2030 | Capitalize on AI and Cloud Demand with Thermo Fisher, Merck, Bio-Rad, and Randox","source":"finance.yahoo.com","link":"https://finance.yahoo.com/healthcare/articles/2026-global-screener-proficiency-testing-143300423.html","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794580380.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.844,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["guidance"]},{"title":"What Bio Rad Laboratories Doubters Overlooked","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/pharmaceuticals-biotech/nyse-bio/bio-rad-laboratories/news/what-bio-rad-laboratories-doubters-overlooked","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794575743.964,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.681,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Bio-Rad COO Jonathan P. DiVincenzo Sells 919 Class A Shares at $390.47 Each","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/news/announcements/bio-rad-coo-jona